=20

For those who may interested, I=E2=80=99ve written up a brief review of my=
=20
experience of this year=E2=80=99s jbonunsla, which took place last weekend =
(August=20
28-30) at the Holiday Inn in Princeton, New Jersey.


I took the train from New York City, arriving in Princeton fairly late=20
Friday night. Pierre Abbat (who had driven up from North Carolina) and Mark=
=20
Shoulson (=E2=80=9Cclsn=E2=80=9D, who lives nearby) had arrived some time b=
efore me, and=20
wandered down to join me in the restaurant just before it closed. I was=20
excited to meet these two, who are the second and third lojbanists whom I=
=20
had ever met in person.


I had corresponded with Pierre over the last year while making some small=
=20
additions to the Lojban Bible translation project that Pierre maintains:=20
https://github.com/phma/lojban-bible


Mark I knew mostly from reading old message board threads where he=20
introduced the =E2=80=9Cdotside=E2=80=9D notation:=20
https://mw.lojban.org/papri/BPFK:_Old_Morphology


I think Pierre was the first to speak lojban: He=E2=80=99s mastered a numbe=
r of=20
lojban tongue twisters. (Pierre, are those collected anywhere?) tsani=20
arrived some time later, having trained down from Montr=C3=A9al, and it was=
 then=20
that we started to jbota=E2=80=99a in earnest. (to ki=E2=80=99e la tsani to=
i)


Saturday morning, I found Pierre already at work preparing the meeting=20
room. He had created construction paper letters using the initials B=20
(be=E2=80=99a), D (du=E2=80=99a), N (ne=E2=80=99a), V (vu=E2=80=99a) to mar=
k the cardinal directions in the=20
room. Suitably oriented, we played a recording of the lojban anthem (
https://mw.lojban.org/papri/Lojban_Anthem) to mark the beginning of the=20
conference. Welcome to lojbanistan! fi=E2=80=99i do=E2=80=99o


Pierre brought his concertina, =E2=80=9CTina=E2=80=9D, and I brought my gui=
tar, so we=20
played a few songs, including bits from Djemynai=E2=80=99s album (e.g. =C2=
=ABnasa=C2=BB) and=20
some songs Pierre had translated such as =C2=ABdoi cevni do'u .au mi jbize'=
a do=C2=BB=20
(=E2=80=9CNearer, My God, To Thee=E2=80=9D).


durka42 arrived from Philadelphia in time to join the the CLL edit-a-thon=
=20
which connected, via Skype and IRC, those of us in the Princeton with=20
Robin, Ilmen, mezohe and others. We managed to review quite a few chapters,=
=20
and to iterate with Robin on the solution to some widespread formatting=20
issues. durka incorporated the latest changes in the automatic build that=
=20
he hosts: http://alexburka.com/lojban/prince.php


Work on preparing CLL for reprint continues, as can be seen by the list of=
=20
issues on github: https://github.com/lojban/cll/issues


Brian Wernham of NooLearn, who had just flown in from London, arrived=20
towards the end of the edit-a-thon, and helped us eat some delivery pizza.=
=20
Then lojbab and noras logged into the #lojban channel on IRC for a chat in=
=20
lojban and English.


At some point during the chat, we played =C2=ABlo jbobau cu mo=C2=BB (
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DN-Y9eBBTrxU) and Djemynai=E2=80=99s newes=
t song,=20
=C2=ABtensa=C7=90a=C2=BB (https://djemynai.bandcamp.com/album/tensaia) and =
clsn opined=20
that lojban, with it=E2=80=99s comparatively short words and rhyme and stre=
ss=20
patterns is well-suited for rap.


Nora was asked if she had anything to do with =C2=ABfanmo jimte=C2=BB, a re=
markable=20
film on Youtube (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DJ_nKJW_KuK4) which is=20
narrated in lojban and attributed to a =E2=80=9CNora Lee=E2=80=9D. Nora sai=
d that was=20
someone else. (On Sunday, I found some more information about the film and=
=20
added it to the Lojban timeline:=20
https://mw.lojban.org/papri/Lojban_timeline#2008)


tsani had the attendees at the conference sign his copy of la xuncku. One=
=20
of the attendees included a message written in srilermorna script (
https://mw.lojban.org/papri/srilermorna).


After dinner, we watched and discussed videos in lojban including =C2=ABlo =
cizra=20
zarci=C2=BB (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3DMmtQ0NcVFjI) an original sto=
ry in=20
lojban by tsani, read by selpa=E2=80=99i, and a few episodes of bripre jikc=
a (
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3Dc6fXtgkPdWQ). We also had to bid farewell=
=20
to durka42.


Activities commenced after breakfast on Sunday morning with an=20
international chat via Mumble, which included a few rounds of =C2=ABla re n=
o=20
preti=C2=BB. Good fun.


Then, with Brian=E2=80=99s assistance, attendees in Princeton and elsewhere=
 via=20
Internet (e.g. selpa=E2=80=99i, Ilmen, Broca) signed into NooLearn=E2=80=99=
s servers (
https://noolearn-staging.herokuapp.com/) to write and review hundreds of=20
questions in and about Lojban, renewing efforts to devise a Lojban=20
proficiency exam. As a follow-up, Pierre has asked for volunteers to join=
=20
LBCK (lojbo bangu cipra kamni) to continue this work:=20
https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/lojban-lbck


We said farewell to Brian Sunday afternoon, and I returned to Brooklyn=20
Sunday evening.


I was glad to have the opportunity to meet lojbanists in person, and to=20
participate in exchanges between lojbanists of many generations, including=
=20
founders, and those around the world. I was also inspired by revisiting and=
=20
sharing the songs, stories and videos that people have created with lojban,=
=20
and by the work of people contributing to efforts such as LBCK and the=20
republication of CLL.


Thank you to Pierre for taking the initiative to organize jbonunsla, to=20
Brian for helping to sponsor jbonunsla, and for making NooLearn available=
=20
to us as a resource, to Robin for heading up the CLL edit-a-thon, to Bob=20
and Nora for joining us in IRC, and to everyone else who attended or=20
participated in jbonunsla. If anyone else would like to contribute towards=
=20
the cost of jbonunsla, please feel free to do so via LLG=E2=80=99s PayPal d=
onation=20
form: http://tiki.lojban.org/tiki/Donations


=E2=80=94Riley

mu=E2=80=99o mi=E2=80=99e la mukti

